                    "A complete acquisition checklist is a sequence of questions that prevents the price, the enthusiasm, or the contract deadline from replacing diligence. It begins with the intended use and ends with a documented decision to proceed, revise, or stop. The checklist should cover legal description, title and liens, survey, boundaries, access, zoning and permits, environmental and physical conditions, utilities, taxes and insurance, neighbors, contracts, and the seller's claims. Some items require a qualified local professional; the investor's role is to ask the right question, obtain the evidence, and understand what remains unknown.",
                    "Separate confirmed facts from representations and assumptions. A seller may state that the road is legal, but the investor should obtain the recorded document and confirm the responsible party. A county map may show a zone, but it may not establish every development right. A well or septic concept may be encouraging, but it may not apply to the proposed use or season. Record the source, date, author, and limitations of each item. A checklist that says verified without defining verification can create false comfort. Use statuses such as document received, professional review complete, estimate only, and open question.",
                    "The checklist must connect diligence to the purchase decision. A low purchase price cannot compensate for an unquantified title defect, impossible access, or an approval path that does not exist. Conversely, a minor issue that has a clear remedy and a known cost may be manageable. Rank issues by probability, severity, time to resolve, and cost to cure, but do not treat a ranking as a legal conclusion. Ask what evidence would change the rank and who is responsible for obtaining it. Set a deadline and a funding source for each critical item before signing or closing.",
